{"qid":"Q205662","names":["Tokugawa Shogunate"],"title":"Tokugawa Shogunate","endonym":{"native":"徳川幕府","lang":"ja"},"capsule":"The Tokugawa (Edo) Shogunate was a hereditary military government of Japan founded by Tokugawa Ieyasu following his victory at Sekigahara in 1600 and his receipt of the shogunal title in 1603. For over two and a half centuries it maintained domestic peace through a system of alternate attendance (sankin-kōtai) that kept daimyo lords subordinate, a strict social hierarchy, and the sakoku policy restricting foreign trade to Dutch and Chinese merchants at Nagasaki. The shogunate ended when Commodore Perry's 1853 arrival exposed its military weakness; Shogun Yoshinobu resigned power to Emperor Meiji in 1867 and the Boshin War of 1868–69 consolidated the imperial restoration.","attestation":"Boundaries from Seshat Cliopatria (Bennett et al., Scientific Data 2025; CC BY 4.0), digitized from standard historical atlases at ~0.07° resolution. Territorial extent is very well attested through extensive administrative records, domain cadastres, and official maps; the shogunate's domain boundaries and vassal territories are among the best-documented in pre-modern East Asian history.","attestationLinks":[{"label":"Cliopatria dataset (Seshat, CC BY 4.0)","url":"https://github.com/Seshat-Global-History-Databank/cliopatria"},{"label":"Bennett et al. 2025, Scientific Data","url":"https://www.nature.com/articles/s41597-025-04516-9"}],"citations":[{"label":"Britannica: Tokugawa period","url":"https://www.britannica.com/topic/Tokugawa-period"},{"label":"Britannica: Tokugawa shogunate","url":"https://www.britannica.com/topic/Tokugawa-shogunate"}],"britannica":"topic/Tokugawa-shogunate","cowCode":null,"wikipedia":"Tokugawa shogunate","rulers":[{"qid":null,"name":"Tokugawa Ieyasu","nativeName":"徳川家康","roman":"Tokugawa Ieyasu","start":1603,"end":1605,"image":null,"imageUrl":null,"imageLicense":null},{"qid":null,"name":"Tokugawa Hidetada","nativeName":"徳川秀忠","roman"